# Public sites: an app reachable from OUTSIDE your home network at https://<your domain>:<port>,
# with a Let's Encrypt certificate. The local sites in the Caddyfile (server IP + Caddy's own
# CA) are untouched — at home, everything keeps working exactly as before.
#
# One snippet per app, named public_<app dir>, taking your domain as {args[0]}. Nothing here is
# served by itself: `hsctl cert` writes domain.caddy — `import public_vaultwarden <domain>` for
# each app you chose to make public — once the certificate exists. The certificate is
# <repo>/letsencrypt/certificates/<domain>.crt/.key, mounted read-only at /letsencrypt; hsctl
# renews it and reloads Caddy. See docs/letsencrypt.md.
#
# The dashboard has no public site on purpose: it's a root shell on the server.
# Adding an app? Give it a public_<dir> snippet too, mirroring its site in the Caddyfile.

(public_vaultwarden) {
	{args[0]}:{$VAULT_HTTPS} {
		bind 0.0.0.0
		tls /letsencrypt/certificates/{args[0]}.crt /letsencrypt/certificates/{args[0]}.key
		import vault
	}
}

(public_nextcloud) {
	{args[0]}:{$CLOUD_HTTPS} {
		bind 0.0.0.0
		tls /letsencrypt/certificates/{args[0]}.crt /letsencrypt/certificates/{args[0]}.key
		import cloud
	}
}

